<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siem Reap</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Siem</a> Reap is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, capital of Siem Reap Province, near the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. The province was ruled by Siam from the late 18th century until the Franco-Siamese treaty of 1907 returned it to Cambodia under French protection. The city grew as the gateway to Angkor and is now the centre of Cambodia's tourism industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siem-reap.jpg" alt="Siem Reap" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willuconquer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_porch_of_Wat_Damnak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Siem Reap is the capital and largest city of Siem Reap province in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and the second-largest city of the country. It lies close to the ancient temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, built during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, and about 30 minutes north of the Tonlé Sap, the great lake and river system that is central to Cambodia. It has a tropical wet and dry climate, with a wet season from May to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> period the site was probably occupied by a town called Dvijendrapura, whose position may be marked by the old temple of Prah Einkosei. A stele there, inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-khmer">Old Khmer</a> and dated to 968 and 970 CE, records endowments made by the princess Indralakṣmī and her husband Divākara-bhaṭṭa.</p><p>The name Siem Reap is commonly translated as defeat of Siam. According to oral tradition King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ang-chan-i">Ang Chan I</a> (1516–1566) gave it after repulsing a Siamese army sent by King Maha Chakkraphat in 1549. Scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a> regard this as a modern folk etymology and hold that the origin of the name is unknown. Sources date the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> to more than a century earlier, when a military expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> captured and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> in 1431, beginning a long period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Siem Reap, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battambang">Battambang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisophon">Sisophon</a>, was under Siamese administration from 1795 until 1907, and the provinces were known collectively as Inner Cambodia.</p><p>Siem Reap was little more than a village when French explorers such as Henri Mouhot reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in the 19th century, although European visitors had seen the ruins earlier, among them António da Madalena in 1586. In 1901 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> began its long work of clearing and restoring the site, and the first Western tourists arrived that year. Following a Franco-Siamese treaty in 1907 the area passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> under French protection and Siem Reap began to grow. The Grand Hotel d'Angkor opened in 1929. In 1975 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> drove the population of Siem Reap into the countryside, as in every Cambodian town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Siem Reap has French-colonial and Chinese-style architecture in the Old French Quarter and around the Old Market. It is the gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> that includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, with its five towers symbolising Mount Meru and its bas-reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> narratives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, the royal city built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> at the end of the 12th century and known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>. Tourism is the centre of the local economy. The Angkor National Museum opened on 12 November 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siem Reap” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French treaties with Siam fixed the Mekong frontier and returned Battambang, <b>Siem Reap</b> and Sisophon to Cambodia in 1907, while the boundaries between the five territories became the international borders of three states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>One suggested route ran 90 km by canal, across the Tonlé Sap lake and up the <b>Siem Reap</b> River; in 2011 Etsuo Uchida and Ichita Shimoda of Waseda University found a shorter canal of 35 km in satellite imagery and believe the Khmer used it instead.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1907 France obtained Battambang and <b>Siem Reap</b>, with Angkor, from Siam.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-cambodia">French protectorate of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
